@page "/data"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Snapshot
@using ExGrid.Docs.Pages.Pivot


<DocsPage Product="ExGrid.Data" Title="Snapshot"
          Lead="The family's immutable data: a copy of a table at one version, held column by column, so a pass over a million rows is a loop over arrays rather than over objects.">

    <DocsSection Title="What a Snapshot is">
        <p>
            <code>ExGrid.Data</code> has no dependency. ExPivot's bundled source reads it, and anything else may: a
            Snapshot is data, not a query engine, and it does not sort, filter, group or aggregate. Text is held as a
            dictionary of codes, money as exact scaled 64-bit integers, dates as clock values.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Six kinds.</strong> Text, Decimal, Double, Integer, Date and Boolean. Every value of a column is of
                its kind.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A Blank in every kind</strong>, kept apart from <code>""</code> and from 0. From objects, a
                <code>null</code> is a Blank. ExPivot shows it as the Item <code>(blank)</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Whole or refused.</strong> Every way in yields a Snapshot or refuses, naming the row and the column.
                No way in yields the rows that happened to read.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Immutable.</strong> A Change Batch makes the next Snapshot, sharing what it did not touch; the one
                before reads exactly as it did.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            <code>dotnet add package ExGrid.Data --prerelease</code>. ExPivot brings it in already.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="From objects">
        <p>
            <code>SnapshotBuilder&lt;T&gt;</code> declares each column once, with a typed accessor: a value is read without
            boxing, and the objects are kept by reference behind their rows (<code>Snapshot.RecordAt</code>). A Double
            column holds its values as they came, non-finite ones included. <code>Key</code> names the Record Key, a Text or
            Integer column whose value tells each record from every other; a Blank key or one carried twice refuses the
            build.
        </p>
        <p>
            To read a Snapshot, take its columns by name and loop over its slices' spans:
            <code>slice.Codes(textColumn)</code>, <code>slice.Decimals(decimalColumn)</code>. <code>ValueAt(row, column)</code>
            reads one value, boxed, for small reads like the grid below.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(SnapshotKinds)" Mud="typeof(SnapshotKindsMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Snapshot/Accounts.cs", "Examples/Snapshot/SnapshotGrid.cs"])">
            Account 4's desk is <code>""</code>, a value; account 6's is a Blank. Account 7's rate is NaN.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="From a CSV, under a Schema">
        <p>
            A <code>CsvSchema</code> says what each column is and how its text is read: the header it goes by, the decimal
            point and thousands separator, the date formats, the words for true, false and Blank. The file is read as bytes,
            straight into the columns, with no string made per cell, and quoting follows RFC 4180.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>A value its kind cannot read, a record of the wrong length, an open quote or invalid text fails the load, naming the row, the column and the line.</li>
            <li>A declared column missing from the header is refused by name; a column the Schema does not declare is skipped.</li>
            <li>UTF-8 is read with or without its byte-order mark; Shift-JIS when the Schema says <code>Encoding = CsvEncoding.ShiftJis</code>.</li>
            <li>
                For a file nobody has described, <code>CsvSchema.SuggestAsync</code> proposes a Schema from its first rows and
                marks every column whose reading is not clear: digits with leading zeros, mixed date formats, a comma that could
                be the decimal point. It is never applied by itself; show it to the user and read the file once they confirm.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(CsvRead)" Mud="typeof(CsvReadMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Snapshot/SnapshotGrid.cs"])">
            Edit the file and read it again: type a letter into a balance, write a date the other way round, or remove the
            Balance header.
        </DocsExample>
        <p>
            In TypeScript, <code>@@ex-grid/data</code> (published with 0.3.0) reads a CSV under the same Schema, written as an
            object, from a <code>Uint8Array</code>, an <code>ArrayBuffer</code> or a <code>Blob</code> such as a file input's
            <code>File</code>:
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>import { readCsvSnapshot } from '@@ex-grid/data';

const file = input.files[0];   // an &lt;input type="file"&gt;'s File
const snapshot = await readCsvSnapshot({
    columns: [
        { name: 'Account', kind: 'text' },
        { name: 'Balance', kind: 'decimal', culture: 'de-DE' },
        { name: 'Opened', kind: 'date', dateFormats: ['dd.MM.yyyy'] },
    ],
    separator: 'semicolon',
    recordKey: 'Account',
}, file, { onProgress: ({ rows }) => { status.textContent = `${rows} rows`; } });</code></pre>
        <p>
            A refusal is a <code>SnapshotError</code> worded as <code>SnapshotException</code> words it, row, column and line
            included: both packages run the same cases, under <code>tests/cases/data/</code>. A culture is a language tag, read
            through the browser's <code>Intl</code>; a date in a calendar other than the Gregorian or the Thai Buddhist, a standard
            date format in a culture, the standard format <code>U</code>, and the era (<code>g</code>) are refused when the Schema is read rather than read some other
            way.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>suggestCsvSchema</code> proposes a Schema for a file nobody has described, as <code>CsvSchema.SuggestAsync</code>
            does, with the same marks in the same words; a <code>Blob</code> is read only as far as the rows sampled. Its
            <code>culture</code> settles what the sample cannot (the decimal point, whether a date has its day first, and the list
            separator that breaks a tie between two separators), and nothing is applied until the Schema is handed to
            <code>readCsvSnapshot</code>:
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>import { readCsvSnapshot, suggestCsvSchema } from '@@ex-grid/data';

const suggestion = await suggestCsvSchema(file, { culture: 'de-DE' });
for (const column of suggestion.columns) {
    for (const mark of column.marks) console.log(column.column.name, mark.doubt, mark.note);
}
// Show the marks to the user, let them change the Schema, then read the file under it.
const snapshot = await readCsvSnapshot(suggestion.schema, file);</code></pre>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="From columns, in slices">
        <p>
            <code>SnapshotColumnsBuilder</code> takes columns a value or a span at a time, for data a reader has parsed
            itself. A text column makes a string only for text it has not seen. <code>CheckpointAsync</code> checks for
            cancellation, reports progress and yields once the slice's budget (30 ms by default) is spent, so a browser keeps
            painting while a million rows load. Every way in takes the same <code>SnapshotLoadOptions</code>; a cancelled load
            builds nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
            A <code>DbDataReader</code> is read by <code>SnapshotDataReaderBuilder</code>, each column by its own type, and an
            Arrow stream by <a href="data/arrow"><code>ExGrid.Data.Arrow</code></a>.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(LoadMillion)" Mud="typeof(LoadMillionMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Snapshot/SnapshotGrid.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Change Batches">
        <p>
            A <code>ChangeBatch</code> carries records added, records changed (found by their Record Key) and keys removed.
            <code>Snapshot.Apply</code> makes the next Snapshot from it, whole or not at all, and returns a
            <code>SnapshotChange</code>: <code>Before</code> and <code>After</code>, the rows <code>Before</code> held that are
            gone (<code>Removed</code>) and the rows <code>After</code> holds that are new (<code>Added</code>). A changed record is
            one of each, at the place of the one it replaced. A reader folds the change into what it computed rather than start
            again; that is how ExPivot stays live.
        </p>
        <p>
            A batch that changes or removes a key the Snapshot does not hold, adds one it does, names a key twice, or carries a
            Blank key is refused by name, and nothing of it is applied.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ChangeBatches)" Mud="typeof(ChangeBatchesMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Snapshot/Accounts.cs", "Examples/Snapshot/SnapshotGrid.cs"])">
            "Open account 1 again" adds a key the Snapshot already holds.
        </DocsExample>
        <p>
            In TypeScript, <code>@@ex-grid/data</code> (published with 0.3.0) applies the same batches:
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>import { applyChangeBatch, createChangeBatch } from '@@ex-grid/data';

const batch = createChangeBatch({ added, changed, removedKeys: [17n] });   // added and changed are Snapshots of records
const { before, after, removed, added: brought } = applyChangeBatch(snapshot, batch);</code></pre>
        <p>
            <code>removed</code> and <code>added</code> are row numbers, ascending, in <code>before</code> and <code>after</code>; a
            changed record keeps its row, and an added one comes at the end. A refusal is a <code>SnapshotError</code> naming the
            row, column and key in the same words as <code>SnapshotException</code>: both packages run the same cases, under
            <code>tests/cases/data/</code>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="From JSON, under a Board Input">
        <p>
            Data a tool returns is usually JSON, which tells no decimal from a double, no integer, and no date. A
            <code>BoardInput</code> names the data and declares each column's kind; <code>FromJson</code> makes a Snapshot by
            that declaration, one column per declared column. A missing member or <code>null</code> is a Blank, and a
            member the input does not declare is not read. A value that does not convert, <code>"abc"</code> as a decimal,
            is refused naming the input, the row and the column, never dropped or read as zero. Kinds are never inferred
            from the rows: a first page of nulls, or <code>1</code> beside <code>1.5</code>, would be guessed wrong.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>Bind</code> and <code>Check</code> hold a source's shape, a <code>DataShape</code>, against the declaration
            before any row is read, and refuse a missing column or one of another kind by name, so a change upstream is
            caught on the run that meets it. <a href="exgrid/columns">Columns built at run time</a> draws such a Snapshot.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

